"13_variable_binding.mst — Dolphin's `#{Namespace.Name}` literal, as an object.

 `#{Core.Array}` denotes the BINDING, not the value: Dolphin's compiler makes a
 `Kernel.VariableBinding` at compile time, and `#{Core.Array} value` answers
 the class. The house dialect has no such literal, so the translator lowers
 each one to `(VariableBinding path: 'Core.Array')` — see
 `tools/dolphin2mst/litarray.py`.

 WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN ITS SIZE SUGGESTS. Dolphin does not build its
 windows in code; it deserialises them. `resource_Default_view` answers a
 literal array whose every class reference is a binding literal:

     ^#(#'!!STL' 6 2118 11 #{UI.STBViewProxy} #{UI.ContainerView} ...
        774 #{Core.MessageSend} #createWindow: 1030 #{UI.CreateWindow} ...)

 The translator used to refuse any method containing `#{`, which meant every
 one of the 654 `resource_*` methods in the corpus was dropped. This class and
 that rewrite are what let a Dolphin window be read from Dolphin's own data.

 NAMESPACES. Dolphin 8 has them; this port does not. A binding therefore keeps
 the FULL dotted path for printing and identity, and resolves on the LAST
 segment — `#{UI.ContainerView}` and a bare `ContainerView` are the same class
 here. Recorded rather than hidden: if two namespaces ever contribute the same
 short name, this is where the collision would surface."
Object subclass: VariableBinding [
    | path cached |

    VariableBinding class >> path: aString [
        ^self basicNew setPath: aString
    ]
    "Dolphin also spells it `#{X} binding` in places; the binding of a binding
     is itself."
    binding [ ^self ]

    setPath: aString [ path := aString. cached := nil ]
    path [ ^path ]

    "The last dotted segment — the name this port knows the class by."
    name [
        | i |
        i := 0.
        1 to: path size do: [ :k | (path at: k) = $. ifTrue: [ i := k ] ].
        ^i = 0 ifTrue: [ path ] ifFalse: [ path copyFrom: i + 1 to: path size ]
    ]
    key [ ^self name asSymbol ]

    "`value` is a universal helper (CLAUDE.md rule 2), and that is fine here:
     the helper's slow path ATTEMPTS the real send first, so a defined
     `value` is reached — which is how ValueHolder and the STB proxies work.
     Checked with tools/audit_helpers.py rather than assumed."
    value [
        ^cached ifNil: [ cached := Worker classNamed: self name asSymbol ]
    ]
    isDefined [ ^self value notNil ]

    "Dolphin's `setValue:` writes THROUGH the binding to the global. This port
     has no writable global table reachable from here, so it is refused loudly
     rather than silently dropping the write — a silent no-op here would
     corrupt whatever was being initialised. No caller in the translated wave
     uses it; if one appears, that is the moment to add the table."
    setValue: anObject [
        ^self error: 'VariableBinding>>setValue: not supported — ' , path
    ]

    = anObject [
        ^(anObject isKindOf: VariableBinding) and: [ anObject path = path ]
    ]
    hash [ ^path hash ]

    printOn: aStream [
        aStream nextPutAll: '#{'; nextPutAll: path; nextPut: $}
    ]
]
